   },
   "9": {
    "original": "Two major rivals in Pakistan have emerged to battle the Taliban in Quetta, scene of Tuesday's event blamed on the Taliban.",
    "rewrite": "In Pakistan, two main competitors have arisen to challenge the Taliban in Quetta, the location of Tuesday's incident attributed to the Taliban."
   },
   "15": {
    "original": "Both Taliban commanders deny the claim that Taliban factions are divided.",
    "rewrite": "Both Taliban commanders refuse to acknowledge that Taliban factions are divided."
   }
  },
  "final_span": "Omar's death.\nIt is reportedly rumored that Mansoor has a close relationship with Pakistan's military and intelligence agencies and the Afghan government, and together they are attempting to involve him in an amnesty plan for those in the insurgency.\n\"This an attempt to break up the Taliban. The leadership is self-inflicting this injury on the group,\" said Yousafzai.\n\"Just when they were close to coming to the table, this injury has torn a hole in the tape and kept the peace process at bay.\"\n\"This news will not bring peace, it will only keep us in war long enough for the rest of my generation and the generations to come suffer the wrath of terrorism.\n\"Our government is trying to reach out to Taliban leadership. If the news is true for Mansoor, then it means the Taliban leadership is further divided.\n\"Whom will they potentially send as their representative if it turns out that their right hand is three hours away from being amputated - after all how what has occurred is the insurgents' attempt to push Mansoor out - there is no success here.\"\nIn Pakistan, two main competitors have arisen to challenge the Taliban in Quetta, the location of Tuesday's incident attributed to the Taliban.\nConspiratorial suspicions about Taliban tensions are so prevalent that on Tuesday, a tweet alleged a confidant of Taliban leader Mullah Mansoor had disclosed Kabul government information to diminish the stature of a competing Taliban group.\nWhile it seemed the Taliban did not verify the night-time meeting invitation and hence faced consequences from the Quetta police, a senior Quetta police official informed Al Jazeera that \"both sides\" had faith in each other.\n\"Mansoor likes a lot of meetings. He likes his own people to discuss the events. So it is nothing new,\" the officer told Al Jazeera.\nBoth Taliban commanders refuse to acknowledge that Taliban factions are divided.\n\"If the news is true, then this is a direct attack of Mansoor's leadership role,\" said Commander Zar Mohammad, who said he fought by",
